1. Core Thesis & Problem Diagnosis
Core Hypothesis: Gurugram's recurring monsoon flooding is an operational failure rather than a budget failure. Shifting the Municipal Corporation of Gurugram (MCG) from reactive pumping to a 24–48 hour predictive drainage workflow can reduce waterlogging across Gurugram's 159 identified waterlogging hotspots.
 * Extreme Rainfall Impact: On September 1, 2025, 133 mm of rain in four hours triggered a 20 km traffic jam and a 51-hour power outage across the city.
 * Pre-Monsoon Desilting Lag: By late June 2026, MCG had completed desilting at only 16 of 36 identified hotspots against a May 31 deadline, despite a mandate to desilt 607 km of drainage network.
 * Why Current Operations Fall Short: Maintenance is reactive, field status relies on manual calls and site visits, and crew or pump allocation lacks hydrological risk modelling across all 159 hotspots.
